 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DffkUlCiTYW/",
 "title": "Likability and restraint as competitive advantage with AI",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "Artificial intelligence allows competitors to easily match technical knowledge and produce polished proposals quickly. The creator claims that when competence is equal, likability and making people enjoy working with you becomes a powerful competitive advantage. A key tactic when disagreeing is to ask what led someone to their conclusion rather than immediately correcting them.",
 "key_points": [
  "AI allows competitors to look just as smart, knowledgeable, and efficient as you by producing proposals and presentations quickly.",
  "Likability is not about being charming or telling jokes, but about making people genuinely enjoy dealing with you.",
  "People prefer working with someone who makes them feel comfortable expressing opinions, asking foolish questions, or admitting uncertainty.",
  "When someone expresses an opinion you disagree with, do not immediately show why you know better.",
  "Instead ask: That's interesting, what led you to that conclusion? Then listen.",
  "Asking questions first makes the other person feel their perspective matters, which is rare with accomplished professionals.",
  "Knowing when not to immediately give a brilliant AI-prepared answer is a human skill that creates competitive advantage."
 ],
 "content": "Artificial intelligence is making it easier for your competition to look just as smart and knowledgeable as you do. I mean, they can produce impressive proposals, polished presentations, and persuasive marketing materials in a fraction of the time that it once took. So, here's an interesting question. When everyone appears equally competent, what makes someone want to work with you? Now, increasingly, I believe one of the answers is something we don't talk about nearly enough, likability. And I don't mean being charming, telling jokes, or having an outgoing personality. I mean making people genuinely enjoy the experience of dealing with you. So I mean think about two people who are equally qualified to help you with an important decision. One is knowledgeable, efficient, and always has a ready answer. The other is just as knowledgeable but there's something different about the conversations. You feel comfortable expressing an opinion asking what seem like a foolish question or admitting that you're uncertain about something. Which person would you rather work with? Now here's a tip that isn't particularly obvious. When someone expresses an opinion you disagree with don't immediately demonstrate why you know better. Instead ask that's interesting. What led you to that conclusion? Then listen. You may still disagree but you've done something important first. You made the person feel that their perspective matters. And that's increasingly rare, particularly when dealing with highly a professionals who are accustomed to being the experts in the conversation. AI can help you prepare a brilliant answer but knowing when not to immediately give that answer is a human skill that can make you considerably more enjoyable to work with and in a world where technical competence is becoming easier to demonstrate, that can be a very powerful competitive advantage. So, if you like more practical ideas for attracting and winning affluent go ahead and comment more and I'll send you information about all my books, my programs, and even how to work with me personally. This is Mark Satterfield. Hope you found this helpful. I'll talk to you soon. Bye for now.",
 "status": "done",
